nmap is a powerful network scanner that can be used to automate node and port discovery within a network. Given a subnet, it can scan to find devices connected, open ports, and what's listening on them.

First task is to discover live hosts. You can do this by specifying an IP range in the following ways:
    use - to specify an ip range: 192.168.0.1-10
    use / to specify a subnet: 192.168.0.1/24
    specify the hostname of a specific system on the network

run on root to avoid being restricted to icmp and tcp connect scans

-sn will scan for live hosts and not attempt to figure out what's listening on them. This is extremely quiet, but limited on the information it will return

on a local network, nmap starts by sending arp requests and labels responses with 'host is up'

on a remote network, you do not get the MAC addresses only the IPs of up devices

-PS/PA/PU[PORTLIST] sends TCP SYN/ACK/UDP respectively to the given list of ports

-sL will return a list of targets it plans to scan for without actually running the scan, verifying that your list is correct before launch

At this point, we know who's online but we don't know who's listening. Enter port scanning.

nmap's connect scan essentially tries to telnet to every port.  This gets triggered with the -sT. T for TCP- because it's trying to complete a TCP 3way handshake with each port. This creates a lot of logs, so it's not considered stealthy

-sS only sends the Syn packet during discovery, so it's considered stealthy because it doesn't generate many logs. You can see on wireshark that if the port is open, it will respond with an Ack packet, in which case nmap responds back with a RST ACK packet. If the port's closed, the target responds with a RST ACK packet initially.

-sU to scan for UDP services. Since it's connectionless, you'll either see success or destination unreachable

By default, nmap only scans the most common 1000 ports

-F (Fast) only scans the 100 most common
-P[range] Allows you to specify a range to scan
-p- scans all ports (1-65535)

*Tip- use -p1-1023 to scan for the ports 1-1024 aka the well-known ports

Now if you want more information about your hosts, use the -O option. It's not conclusive, but it does make an educated guess about the host OS.

-sV enables version detection- You've discovered several open ports and want to know what services are running on them.

-A is basically advanced. It enables OS detection, version scanning, traceroute, and a few other things.

-Pn tells nmap to scan the ports even on hosts that were marked as offline during discovery

To get more verbose with timing and timeouts:
--min-rate
--max-rate
--host-timeout

Controlling Output:
-V is obviously for verbosity
  This flag is useful when you want to monitor real-time progress as nmap is operating, like if you run a T0 and want to ensure that it's still running properly the next day.
-d is for debugging output. You can specify up to 4 'v's or 9 'd's. When looking at debugging level output, it's useful to note that you'll get thousands of lines of extremely low level output. Useful for RE and complicated exploits, or network engineers troubleshooting in depth.

To store output in a file:
-oN = Normal output
-oX = XML file output
-oG = grepable output
-oA = all of the above major formats
syntax:
    -o<> <filename>
    nmap -sS -t2 -oG <filename> <IP>